UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 8: Aural skills and listening', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A listening map puts a sound story in time order: what happens first, what happens next, and what happens last. This lesson uses an original four-event story, so your map needs four boxes, not a single picture of the loudest moment. Silence can be one of the four events. Skipping it breaks the sequence a performer would follow. You may draw simple marks or write a phrase in each box. Either way, another person should be able to replay the story from your page. Order matters more than decoration, because a map is a timeline.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nMap this different story from start to finish: a high hum, then four fast claps, then one loud stamp, then silence. What belongs in first, next, later, and last?\n\nFEEDBACK\nA strong answer keeps the high hum first, the four fast claps next, the loud stamp later, and the silence last. Each box names the sound, not a mood alone. Silence is last, so it is not dropped for being quiet and not moved earlier. The stamp is loud, but loudness does not earn the first box. A reader who performs your map should stamp only after the claps.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThe loudest event should be drawn first because it is the most important, even when it happened last. That fails because a listening map follows time, not a ranking of excitement. Put the loud stamp, ring, or shout in the box that matches when you heard it. Importance can be noted in words inside the correct box, without moving the box.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nDraw or describe a four-box map for this new story: four spoon taps on a cup, then a whispered \"shhh\", then a wooden chair scrape, then one metal-lid ring. Label the boxes first, next, later, and last. Do not reuse the finger clicks, the door-knocks, the high hum, or the stamp story. Include the whisper even though it is soft.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Music","learning_intention":"A listening map puts a sound story in time order: what happens first, what happens next, and what happens last. This lesson uses an original four-event story, so your map needs four boxes, not a single picture of the loudest moment. Silence can be one of the four events. Skipping it breaks the sequence a performer would follow. You may draw simple marks or write a phrase in each box. Either way, another person should be able to replay the story from your page. Order matters more than decoration, because a map is a timeline.","lesson_number":8,"lesson_role":"Apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MU4-LIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Draw or describe a four-box map for this new story: four spoon taps on a cup, then a whispered \"shhh\", then a wooden chair scrape, then one metal-lid ring. Label the boxes first, next, later, and last. Do not reuse the f Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using order on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: The loudest event should be drawn first because it is the most important, even when it happened last.. Correction: A strong answer keeps the high hum first, the four fast claps next, the loud stamp later, and the silence last.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: The loudest event should be drawn first because it is the most important, even when it happened last. That fails because a listening map follows time, not a ranking of excitement. Put the loud stamp, ring, or shout in the box that matches when you heard it. Importance can be noted in words inside the correct box, without moving the box. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating order on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with order, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about order with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for order. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for order. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["The loudest event should be drawn first because it is the most important, even when it happened last","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work","A strong answer keeps the high hum first, the four fast claps next, the loud stamp later, and the silence last"],"question":"Map this different story from start to finish: a high hum, then four fast claps, then one loud stamp, then silence. What belongs in first, next, later, and last?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for order; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["The loudest event should be drawn first because it is the most important, even when it happened last","A listening map puts a sound story in time order: what happens first, what happens next, and what happens last","A strong answer keeps the high hum first, the four fast claps next, the loud stamp later, and the silence last","Careful use of map with clear evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with order?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named map, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["The loudest event should be drawn first because it is the most important, even when it happened last","Name map only, without linking it to order","Apply order to new material and name the map used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies order with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on order. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["The loudest event should be drawn first because it is the most important, even when it happened last","A strong answer keeps the high hum first, the four fast claps next, the loud stamp later, and the silence last","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance"],"question":"Which response best evaluates elements, techniques and audience effect when analysing order?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","The loudest event should be drawn first because it is the most important, even when it happened last","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for order","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about order?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (A listening map puts a sound story in time order: what happens first) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Story in order: first, three soft finger clicks","The loudest event should be drawn first because it is the most important, even when it happened last","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about order?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete"],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for order?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example","The loudest event should be drawn first because it is the most important, even when it happened last","Name map only, with no linked example","Draw or describe a four-box map for this new story: four spoon taps on a cup, then a whispered \"shhh\", then a wooden"],"question":"Which independent response best applies order to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about order with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating order on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using order on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for order. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with order, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: The loudest event should be drawn first because it is the most important, even when it happened last.. Correction: A strong answer keeps the high hum first, the four fast claps next, the loud stamp later, and the silence last.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-music-y9-w04-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 4 alignment to Music 7–10 (2024) outcome MU4-LIS-01. Content-point mapping is pending. Not NESA verified. No copyrighted recordings are required; examples are original descriptions or student-made sounds.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise order.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Music","success_criteria":["I can use order with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify map and explain why it matters.","I can practise event independently on a new prompt.","I can notice sequence needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A listening map puts a sound story in time order: what happens first, what happens next, and what happens last.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason","A listening map puts a sound story in time order: what happens first, what happens next, and what happens last","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about order?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nStory in order: first, three soft finger clicks; next, two door-knocks close together; later, about two counts of silence; last, a low hum that fades away. Box 1 gets the clicks, box 2 the knocks, box 3 the silence, and box 4 the fading hum. The silence stays in the third box even though it is quiet, because it is an event with duration. The hum is last, so it does not jump to the front for being easier to notice. Read the four boxes aloud in order and check that nothing swapped places.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMap this different story from start to finish: a high hum, then four fast claps, then one loud stamp, then silence. What belongs in first, next, later, and last?\n\nFEEDBACK\nA strong answer keeps the high hum first, the four fast claps next, the loud stamp later, and the silence last. Each box names the sound, not a mood alone. Silence is last, so it is not dropped for being quiet and not moved earlier. The stamp is loud, but loudness does not earn the first box. A reader who performs your map should stamp only after the claps.","explain":"A listening map puts a sound story in time order: what happens first, what happens next, and what happens last. This lesson uses an original four-event story, so your map needs four boxes, not a single picture of the loudest moment. Silence can be one of the four events. Skipping it breaks the sequence a performer would follow. You may draw simple marks or write a phrase in each box. Either way, another person should be able to replay the story from your page. Order matters more than decoration, because a map is a timeline.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"The loudest event should be drawn first because it is the most important, even when it happened last. That fails because a listening map follows time, not a ranking of excitement. Put the loud stamp, ring, or shout in the box that matches when you heard it. Importance can be noted in words inside the correct box, without moving the box. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Two boxes show the first sound event beside the next event.","caption":"A listening map starts at the beginning, not the loudest moment.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">First</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Event 1 opens the story</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Next</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Event 2 follows at once</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Story in order: first, three soft finger clicks; next, two door-knocks close together; later, about two counts of silenc Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Story in order: first, three soft finger clicks; next, two door-knocks close together; later, about two counts of silenc Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about order?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nStory in order: first, three soft finger clicks; next, two door-knocks close together; later, about two counts of silence; last, a low hum that fades away. Box 1 gets the clicks, box 2 the knocks, box 3 the silence, and box 4 the fading hum. The silence stays in the third box even though it is quiet, because it is an event with duration. The hum is last, so it does not jump to the front for being easier to notice. Read the four boxes aloud in order and check that nothing swapped places. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Two boxes show the third event, which may be silence, beside the last event.","caption":"Four boxes stay in time order from first to last.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=