stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nA strong answer keeps the high hum first, the four fast claps next, the loud stamp later, and the silence last. Each box names the sound, not a mood alone. Silence is last, so it is not dropped for being quiet and not moved earlier. The stamp is loud, but loudness does not earn the first box. A reader who performs your map should stamp only after the claps.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. The loudest event should be drawn first because it is the most important, even when it happened last. That fails because a listening map follows time, not a ranking of excitement. Put the loud stamp, ring, or shout in the box that matches when you heard it. Importance can be noted in words inside the correct box, without moving the box. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about order?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA listening map puts a sound story in time order: what happens first, what happens next, and what happens last. This lesson uses an original four-event story, so your map needs four boxes, not a single picture of the loudest moment. Silence can be one of the four events. Skipping it breaks the sequence a performer would follow. You may draw simple marks or write a phrase in each box. Either way, another person should be able to replay the story from your page. Order matters more than decoration, because a map is a timeline. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. 